By \Cref{lemma:UQS_of_F1}, the stiff well-posedness of \cref{eq} ensures the strong hyperbolicity of reduced systems presented in \cref{eq:reduced}. Given arbitrary $\mu > 0$ and $t > 0$, let us take a sufficiently large $\Xi > 0$ such that  egin{displaymath} \sup_{\eta \ge 0} 
orm{  ig( \hat{u}(t, \cdot, \eta) - \exp(H_{11} t) \hat{u}_0  ig) \mathbbm{1}_{|\cdot| > \Xi} }_{L^2} <  rac{1}{2} \mu, \end{displaymath} according to \Cref{lemma:high_frequency}. By \Cref{lemma:low_frequency}, there exists a constant $\Theta > 0$ such that the following inequality holds for each $\eta > \Theta$:  egin{displaymath} 
orm{  ig( \hat{u}(t, \cdot, \eta) - \exp(H_{11} t) \hat{u}_0  ig) \mathbbm{1}_{|\cdot| \le \Xi} }_{L^2} <  rac{1}{2} \mu. \end{displaymath} At this time, one can obtain that  egin{displaymath} 
orm{  ig( \hat{u}(t, \cdot, \eta) - \exp(H_{11} t) \hat{u}_0  ig) }_{L^2} < \mu, \end{displaymath} which yields the desired convergence. Furthermore, if the matrix set $\mathcal{F}_2$ is uniformly quasi-stable and the initial data satisfies $U_0 \in H^2$, then the error estimate at the low-frequency region with the frequency cutoff $\Xi(\eta) = 	ilde{ eta}^{-1} \eta - 1$ can be given by \cref{eq:low_frequency_error} in \Cref{lemma:low_frequency}. At the high-frequency region, the error can be bounded by  egin{displaymath} 
orm{  ig( \hat{u}(t, \cdot, \eta) - \exp(H_{11} t) \hat{u}_0  ig) \mathbbm{1}_{|\cdot| > \Xi(\eta)} }_{L^2} \le  rac{C}{1 + \Xi(\eta)^2} \|U_0\|_{H^2}. \end{displaymath} Combining the high- and low-frequency estimates, we complete the proof of this theorem.

